{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "254",
    "case_summary": "A young woman presented to the emergency department with a 3-day history of left-sided neck pain, neck stiffness, headache, and associated pyrexia. She denied experiencing dysphagia, dyspnea, and visual disturbances. She had no preceding illnesses and no history of neck trauma. Medical history was noncontributory, and she was taking no regular medications.\n\nClinical examination revealed:- Intact cranial nerves- Firm swelling in the posterior triangle of her left neck - Torticollis\n\n- Laboratory tests:  * White blood cell count: 17 × 109  * C-reactive protein: 87 mg/L  * Cerebrospinal fluid examination: normal  * Microbiological culture: F necrophorum infection, sensitive to co-amoxiclav- Imaging studies:  1. CT with contrast:     Title: Computed Tomographic (CT) Images     Description: Coronal and sagittal CT images showing poorly defined collection in left paraspinal musculature, approximately 6 cm in largest dimensions    2. MRI:     Title: Magnetic Resonance Images (MRIs)     Description: Sagittal and coronal MRIs demonstrating intramuscular abscess in the left levator scapulae muscle extending from approximate muscle origin at C1 to C5 level, measuring 6.5 × 2.0 × 0.8 cm (craniocaudal × anteroposterior × transverse diameter). No evidence of associated osteomyelitis or discitis.",
